WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.850
Hey Thoughty2 Here.

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.670
We all know the old saying “crime doesn’t
pay”.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.870
But once you’ve started down the path of
a criminal lifestyle, it can be pretty hard

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.750
to get out of, since it’s not like you can
just write your boss a letter of resignation

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.480
or go and tell the police that you’re retiring.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.230
But, not only is it possible, some prolific
criminals have even gone on to become very

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:23.630
successful in some other field of mainstream
work.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.400
So let’s have a look at the top seven criminals
who left their life of crime behind and became

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.300
successful legitimately.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:40.600
We’re starting with a superstar, a man recognized
all around the world for his music, his business

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.489
empire and for being part of one of the world’s
most famous couples.

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:49.610
It is, of course, Sean Carter, more commonly
known as Jay Z.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:55.340
These days he’s estimated to be worth around
$500 million and has a finger in so many pies

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.250
he may as well own a bakery.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.580
As one of the greatest rappers of all time,
he’s sold over 100 million records but he

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.719
also runs a record label, a clothing brand,
a fragrance line and even a sports agency.

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:13.719
You’d think he’d just retire and play
golf or something but no, he keeps on going.

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:19.340
It’s more likely that he just has that entrepreneurial
spirit, and that never dies out.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:24.289
So where did he learn this, where did he get
his impressive business skills?

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:30.369
Well as a teenager, he was a drug dealer,
known in his neighbourhood as Jazzy.

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:34.719
The projects he came from were gripped by
the crack cocaine epidemic and young Sean

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:40.909
saw it as one of the few opportunities available
to him as a way to improve his situation.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:44.950
He says that later in his life he looked back
and realised the effects the drugs had on

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.359
the community but at the time he was just
a kid thinking about surviving and buying

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:50.359
clothes.

00:01:50.359 --> 00:01:54.719
So he took it upon himself to learn business
on the streets.

00:01:54.719 --> 00:02:01.099
Jay Z says he owes most of his business skills
and his ability to budget, thanks to his experiences

00:02:01.099 --> 00:02:04.179
dealing drugs on the streets of New York in
the 1980s.

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:08.459
I guess working out a budget for a drug deal
probably makes you learn a lot quicker than

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:14.070
sitting in a classroom listening to your business
professor monotonously explaining spreadsheets.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:18.099
His past is a big part of his music and he
has plenty of great songs about it such as

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:23.459
Friend or Foe from his first album Reasonable
Doubt and then 4th of December on The Black

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:27.489
Album which gives a perfect overview of his
rags to riches story.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:31.150
I wonder if he’ll ever release an album
about how to sit through board meetings, or

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.010
the best way to manage an investment portfolio.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.840
Perhaps he could call it “The Green Album
($)”.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.450
From one of the coolest men on the planet,
to one of its truest geeks.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:47.959
Kevin Mitnick was a hacker in the early 90s,
known as both The Condor and The Darkside

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:48.959
Hacker.

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:53.500
He didn’t think of it as hacking though,
he like to call it “social engineering”,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.689
just like how breaking and entering is “alternative
access engineering”.

00:02:57.689 --> 00:03:03.280
He started out “engineering” himself free
rides on the buses around LA after sussing

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.249
out how to bypass the punch card system.

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:10.189
It’s not exactly the most gangster of beginnings
but hey, we’ve all got to start somewhere,

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:11.189
right?

00:03:11.189 --> 00:03:17.840
But he soon graduated to hacking into multi-national
businesses like Pacific Bell, IBM, Nokia and

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.840
Motorola.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.180
In 1988 he was convicted for 12 months for
breaking into the Digital Equipment Corporation’s

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.209
network and copying their software.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:33.180
But this bad boy didn’t hang up his keyboard
and was arrested in 1995 after almost 3 years

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.180
on the run.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:39.750
It took 4 years to get to trial but he was
ultimately convicted of multiple counts of

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.379
wire fraud and computer fraud.

00:03:42.379 --> 00:03:47.730
He did 8 months in solitary confinement as
the prosecution implied that he could “start

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:51.519
a nuclear war by whistling into a payphone.”

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:56.319
His crimes are likely quite exaggerated though
and probably many people involved in the case

00:03:56.319 --> 00:03:59.760
didn’t truly understand the technical side
of things.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.200
Think about how hard your grandma finds it
to send a text and then imagine she’s on

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.609
the jury of a hacking trial, it’s going
to be a long, slow case.

00:04:07.609 --> 00:04:12.409
It’s only in recent years that law enforcement
has grown more accustomed to the intricacies

00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:14.439
of cybercrime.

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:17.799
Eventually though, Kevin Mitnick decided to
use his powers for good.

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:23.470
He became a successful security consultant
for major corporations and the FBI, teaching

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.270
them about the great mysteries of social engineering.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:31.110
Now he’s legitimate, he will never need
to be locked away from his beloved internet

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.180
again.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:37.080
Mitnick now makes a very comfortable living
providing penetration testing services for

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.520
the world’s largest companies.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.870
Now our next man was much harder to catch,
although he did spend a year in prison in

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:45.870
Ohio.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:52.520
He is one of the greatest drivers of all time,
NASCAR legend, Junior Johnson, also nick named

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.590
“the last American hero”.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.550
He was a phenomenal NASCAR driver, winning
50 of 313 races.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:04.440
And he was thrilling to watch, with a hell
for leather style that meant he would either

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.000
walk off with 1st place or just a steering
wheel, if he was lucky.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.590
He retired in his prime, at 35, since the
thrill had gone out of it.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.479
He said "If my car didn't tear up, there wasn't
anyone that could outrun me.”

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:20.050
And he was right.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:21.819
So what was his crime?

00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:27.759
Junior Johnson was a bootlegger and he used
to drive moonshine all around North Carolina,

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:30.919
that’s illegally made booze for those of
you who don’t know.

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:36.349
His father spent a third of his life in prison,
so little Junior Johnson started learning

00:05:36.349 --> 00:05:42.729
from an early age how to outrun the law and
he was a nifty driver by the age of 10, running

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:45.360
up and down the dirt roads near his house.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.389
And it was this reckless driving style that
made him great.

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:54.210
Early on in his career, his car wasn’t as
fast as the others but he was one of the first

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:59.940
drivers to use the technique of going into
the slipstream of a faster car and then sling-shotting

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.220
around it, a practice which is common today.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:09.949
Junior Johnson made it so legit that he even
managed to have his record wiped clean, since,

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:15.919
the day after Christmas in 1986, President
Ronald Reagan gave him a presidential pardon

00:06:15.919 --> 00:06:18.500
for his old days as a moonshine runner.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.860
To be fair, it was boxing day, so the president
was probably feeling pretty good about the

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:28.050
idea of someone bringing him more booze, no
matter where it came from.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:33.160
Running illegal booze has a sort of romantic
charm to it but once you get into harder stuff,

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.610
the magic sort of disappears.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:40.300
Now if I asked you what actor Tim Allen, was
doing in Kalamazoo with some magical white

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.379
powder, you’d maybe think he was filming
another sequel to The Santa Clause.

00:06:44.379 --> 00:06:51.050
But in October 1978, at the airport, he was
actually arrested for transporting over half

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.850
a kilo of cocaine.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:58.449
That’s right, the voice of Buzz Lightyear,
the squeaky clean space ranger, was a drug

00:06:58.449 --> 00:06:59.500
dealer.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:06.070
Allen had just started out as a stand-up comedian,
but smuggling 650g of coke is no joke and

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:09.289
he was staring down the barrel of a life sentence.

00:07:09.289 --> 00:07:13.789
He sensibly decided that he didn’t want
to spend to infinity and beyond behind bars

00:07:13.789 --> 00:07:18.750
and took up the cop’s offer of becoming
an informant and got a reduced sentence of

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:23.139
3-7 years and eventually just served 28 months.

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:27.389
When he came out, he returned to stand-up
comedy and went on to become one of the biggest

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:30.940
names in television with his show Home Improvements.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:37.210
He was so big at one point that he set an
impressive record of having, in one week,

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:42.970
the top rated show – Home Improvements,
the biggest grossing film – The Santa Clause,

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:48.210
and the New York Times best-selling book – Don’t
Stand To Close To A Naked Man.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.409
He probably thought all his Christmases had
come at once.

00:07:51.409 --> 00:07:56.199
He’s estimated to be worth over $80 million
dollars these days, so it’s safe to say

00:07:56.199 --> 00:08:00.889
that crime doesn’t pay, voicing little toy
spacemen does though.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:05.970
Our next criminal was also an actor, but not
the kind who does movies.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:12.370
Frank Abagnale was one of the world’s greatest
conmen and, among many other roles, fraudulently

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:17.629
played the part of a doctor, a lawyer and
an airline pilot.

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:22.680
If this all sounds slightly familiar, it’s
probably because his life was immortalised

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:28.160
on the big screen in the movie Catch Me If
You Can, starring Leonardo Di Caprio and Tom

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.069
Hanks, directed by Steven Spielberg.

00:08:31.069 --> 00:08:37.930
He was born in 1948 in New York and by fifteen
he had begun his first con; on his dad.

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:42.880
Some of us might have stolen a beer or two
out the fridge or helped “lose” a bit

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:51.380
of cash lying around the house but Frank Jnr
took Frank Snr for $3,400 by using his credit

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:54.470
card to buy car parts and then cashing them
in.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:58.800
He said it was for date money, but that’s
an awful lot of popcorn.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.900
Soon he graduated from defrauding the bank
of dad and started going after the real ones

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:09.640
by editing cheques, creating fake identities
and even putting his own account details on

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:16.000
blank deposit slips, so customers would accidentally
place their money into his account.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.450
The name “conman” is short for “confidence”
man and it’s really this ability that made

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.790
him so good at what he did.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:29.300
Often the idea itself was so simple but just
doing it with authority and confidence meant

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.390
he would get away with some incredibly simple
scams.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:37.080
In one bank, he noticed that employees from
United Airlines and Hertz would come in with

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.770
ziplocked bags and place them in a drop box.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:46.140
So Frank put an out of order sign on the box
and a note saying to give the money to the

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.890
security guard.

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.530
Guess who was hiding behind that security
uniform?

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:57.450
After a long chase by the police and a lengthy
list of fake professions, Frank was finally

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:01.840
caught in France, still only 21 years old.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.870
So, how did he turn his life around?

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:10.040
Well after 5 years of his 12 year sentence,
the US government released him, on the condition

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.210
that they could use his hard-earned expertise
to help them track other fraudsters.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:20.130
He eventually ended up acting as a consultant
for major banks, showing them how he had got

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:24.310
around their security and designing better
ways for them to protect themselves.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.650
I don’t know, some people might say that
working for major banks means he hasn’t

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:30.280
really left the conman business after all.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:34.631
A lot of the people we’ve talked about today
are a bit of a surprise, you wouldn’t look

00:10:34.631 --> 00:10:36.490
at Tim Allen and guess drug dealer.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:42.460
But our next guy has made a career as an actor
for the very fact that he kind of looks like

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:45.150
a hardened criminal, and that’s because
he was.

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:52.040
Big, bad Danny Trejo has been playing gangsters
since 1985 and is one of the most prolific

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.940
American actors, performing in at least 4
films a year since the 90s.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:10:59.260
You need a big scary gangster?

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:00.330
Danny’s your man.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:05.660
You’ll see him striding around in many a
Robert Rodriguez or Tarantino film, from Desperado

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.830
to Machete and Dusk Till Dawn.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:12.260
But he’s not just in it for the fame and
money, he’s done plenty of student and independent

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:16.390
projects for just the price of a tank of fuel
and a hot dinner.

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:21.120
So why is Danny Trejo so good at playing a
badass in films?

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.830
Because Danny was a bona fide badass.

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:30.880
He grew up in LA’s echo park and was consistently
in and out of jail for things like armed robbery.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:36.030
He ended up facing the death penalty for throwing
a rock at the head of a police officer, although

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.700
he was aiming at someone else.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:42.500
Whilst in jail he taught himself to box and
went through a drug rehabilitation program

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:48.030
which helped him turn his life around and
become a drug counsellor himself, on leaving

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:49.030
the pen.

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:51.220
And it was counselling that got him into the
movie business.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:56.420
He went to mentor a kid who was struggling
with all the cocaine on set of Runaway Train.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:12:00.710
It turned out the screen writer recognised
him from when they’d done time together

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:06.250
at San Quentin prison and he was asked to
train an actor in boxing and then was given

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.230
a part as a boxer himself.

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:11.350
Who needs LinkedIn when you’ve got prison
buddies?

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:13.950
And prison is still a part of Danny’s life.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:18.390
He’s told journalists that he still sometime
worries he’ll wake up in his cell, with

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.620
the bell ringing for dinner time.

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:23.230
It was all a dream Danny, all a dream.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:29.930
And finally we come to a man who sound tracked
many a romantic evening with his deep, bassy

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:30.930
voice.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:35.720
Barry White is one of the best selling artists
of all time and although he’s famous as

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:44.240
a singer, he actually had his hand in over
130 gold and platinum selling albums and singles.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:50.320
He was a songwriter, composer, producer and
session musician as well a singer.

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:55.550
He worked with bands such as the Banana Splits,
Bob &amp; Earl and Lisa Stansfield as well as

00:12:55.550 --> 00:13:01.110
having his own huge solo hits like the massive
Can't Get Enough of Your Love, Babe.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.560
So what is Barry’s criminal past?

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:08.720
Well, before he was stealing hearts, Barry
White was stealing cars.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:14.390
The great soul singer was born in Texas but
like Danny Trejo, he grew up in the troubled

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:15.510
neighbourhoods of LA.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:20.610
His brother Darryl was murdered in a gang
dispute and Barry rolled into jail at 16 for

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.640
stealing $30,000 of Cadillac tyres.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:28.970
Whilst he was in jail, he heard the famous
Elvis tune “It’s now or never” and he

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:32.970
took the message to heart, deciding to make
a change in his life.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:38.130
He left jail after 4 months and left his life
of crime in the dust, to become the legend

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.470
we know today.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:45.680
He sadly died at just 58, largely since he
was overweight for most of his adult life.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:49.590
So any of you criminals out there looking
for a career change, I hope this has given

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:50.590
you some inspiration.

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:55.360
Or perhaps, after hearing these success stories,
you’re now considering giving up your business

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:56.990
degree to deal drugs.

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:58.730
But please don’t.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:05.130
If these stories tell us anything it’s that
legitimate work pays more than crime – well,

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:15.070
in the long run at least.

